# CivicAI — environment template. Copy to .env.local and fill in.
# Note: .gitignore matches `.env*`, so this file is NOT committed. Keep it
# alongside the repo or add an exception if the team wants it tracked.

# --- Core (citizen side) ---------------------------------------------------
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Unset in development to use the embedded PGlite database (./.data/civicai).
# Required in production.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- AI providers ----------------------------------------------------------
# Every model call — CNIC reading, civic-photo classification, voice
# transcription and complaint drafting — goes through ONE gate
# (src/services/ai/client.ts), so the whole pipeline can be pointed at a
# different vendor by changing this single line:
#
#   AI_PROVIDER=openrouter   free tier, several models per task  (default)
#   AI_PROVIDER=openai       gpt-4o, then gpt-4o-mini  (fastest and most accurate)
#   AI_PROVIDER=gemini       gemini-3.8-flash, then gemini-3.7-flash
#
# The prompts, schemas and validation are identical across all three, so a
# difference in the output is a difference in the MODEL — which is what makes
# comparing them for accuracy worth doing.
#
# Leave it unset and the first provider with a key below wins, in the order
# openrouter, openai, gemini. Set it explicitly and a missing key is an error
# rather than a quiet switch to another vendor: numbers you cannot attribute to
# a provider are worse than no numbers.

# Unset = whichever key below is present wins. Uncomment to force one.
# AI_PROVIDER=openai
# One key per provider. Only the active provider's key is required; keep all
# three and you can switch between them without editing anything else.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Where to check what is actually alive right now:
#
#   npm run check:ai           walks the active provider's chains
#   npm run check:ai -- --all  walks every provider you have a key for
#   npm run check:cnic -- front.jpg   one real CNIC read, on the active provider
#
# No task depends on a single model. Each has an ordered chain and the client
# walks it until one answers usefully, because capacity disappears without
# warning on the free tier. The defaults live in src/services/ai/chains.ts;
# override a chain here (comma-separated, best first, in the ACTIVE provider's
# own model names) if a model is withdrawn mid-demo:
#
#   AI_MODELS_VISION=google/gemma-4-31b-it:free,minimax/minimax-m3:free
#   AI_MODELS_TEXT=gpt-4o-mini
#   AI_MODELS_AUDIO=gemini-3.5-transcribe
#
# NOTE ON AUDIO: exactly one free model on OpenRouter accepts audio input, so
# that chain has no second rung there. OpenAI uses its dedicated transcription
# endpoint instead, and Gemini hears audio in an ordinary chat turn. When
# transcription is unavailable the describe step asks the citizen to type
# instead, which is a first-class path rather than an error.
#
# Rarely needed — a proxy or a regional endpoint:
#   OPENROUTER_BASE_URL= / OPENAI_BASE_URL= / GEMINI_BASE_URL=

# Every request and reply is written to logs/ai/ai-<date>.log (JSON Lines) so a
# bad read can be diagnosed from what was actually sent and received, with the
# provider recorded on each line so a transcript spanning a switch can still be
# read apart. Image and audio payloads are recorded as type and size only,
# never their bytes — but the CNIC transcript DOES contain a citizen's name,
# CNIC number, date of birth and address in clear text, so treat those files
# like a database dump. logs/ is gitignored.
#
#   AI_LOG=off          disable the transcript entirely
#   AI_LOG_DIR=…        write it somewhere other than ./logs/ai
AI_LOG=off          disable the transcript entirely
#   AI_LOG_DIR=…        write it somewhere other than ./logs/ai
AI_LOG=

# --- Government portal -----------------------------------------------------
# false = invite links are printed to the server console (development default)
# true  = deliver over SMTP. The SMTP branch is not implemented yet and throws
#         "SMTP not yet implemented" — deliberately loud, so the flag can never
#         look like working email while nothing is delivered.
EMAIL_ROUTING_ENABLED=false
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=CivicAI <noreply@civicai.local>
